On Tuesday 30 April 2002 00:05, Ray Olszewski wrote:
> At 06:27 PM 4/29/02 +0200, James Mohr wrote:
> >Hi All!
> >
> >I am looking for volunteers to help me with my Linux Tutorial
> >(www.linux-tutorial.info).  Since I cannot program well enough to be
> > involved in any major project, I have decided to make my book "Linux
> > User's Resource" available on the Web as part of my contribution to the
> > Internet community.
>
> [...]
>
> >Because of the scope of this project, I need as much help as I can get.  I
> > am grateful for any contribution you can make. Anything from suggestions
> > for improving the layout or appearance to updating the articles or even
> > writing on new topics will be greatly appreciated.  For those of us who
> > cannot program, but would still like to give something back to the Linux
> > community, I think projects like this provide a great opportunity.
>
> [...]
>
> Since you are not making the material you wrote available under any of the
> GPL-like licenses for documenation (other readers, please see URL
> http://www.linux-tutorial.info/cgi-bin/display.pl?247&0 for the
> copyright/license explanation), it might help if you explained the terms
> under which contributors will be contributing their work to this project.
> If I (or others) write material for the site, who holds the copyright and,
> assuming I (we) do, what rights am I (are we) expected to assign to you?
>
> Please don't take this as an attempt to start an argument about how you
> should make the material available. I don't want to do that; it's your
> material (up to now), and you can make it available on any terms you care
> to. I just want to know the terms under which you are soliticiting the
> volunteer work of others.

Hi Ray!

Thanks for writing!

Not making the information available under a GPL-like license is not my 
decision, but rather my publisher's. There is an FAQ on the site, which goes 
into a few details about it (although I just now put the link to it on the 
home page, it was previously just available from inside.)

The short answer is that contributors keep all of the rights to their 
own original material. Small additions to the tutorial are harder to deal 
with, but I don't see that as a problem as we can work out details between 
us.  My goal here is to make this available from my site for everyone. There 
is little need to make it available using a GPL-like license, as I do not see 
much of a need to copy it (unless you are trying to save on Internet 
connection costs).   However, it was easier to get the permission from the 
publisher to make it available like this rather than freeing it up with a 
GPL-like license.  

The only right that I expect or want is the right to provide the material on 
the web site. You will retain all other rights to the material. If the update 
version is ever published (which is likely), I will make specific, individual 
arragements. Worse case would be simply not to include your material.  
However, wanting to publish the material again, including all of the scripts, 
etc. might be good leverage to convince the publisher to allow us to make it 
available in a GPL-like license. They already have at least one GPL book and 
there are many more from other publishers. 

However, I have already decided that all of the royalties from the updated 
version will go to Jessie's medical fund. As I said, I am doing this project 
as my contribution to the Internet/Linux community. If **we** get the 
material published, it's just a cherry on top as all of the money goes to 
Jessie.  Should Jessie recover and no longer need our direct support, then 
**we** can decide who to donate any money to. 

If you want to contribute, I am more than willing to put any agreement in 
wriritng. We can decide on a some wording whereby you explicitely retain all 
rights. I am willing to work with you to make arrangement that satisfy all of 
us. 

I think it's great that you addressed these topics at the beginning to help 
ease people's fears. Intellectual propery laws are tricky.

Best Regards,

Jim Mohr  
